This simulation explores how an investment’s value might change over five years when annual returns are uncertain. It starts with a hypothetical investment of £1,000 and generates 10,000 possible investment paths.
For this example, annual returns follow a normal distribution with a mean of 7% and a standard deviation of 15%. The mean represents the assumed average annual return, while the standard deviation describes how widely returns vary around that average. These figures are illustrative assumptions, rather than estimates based on market data.
For each path, the code randomly generates a return for each year and updates the investment’s value. Returns compound: each year’s gain or loss applies to the investment’s current value. After repeating this process, the code calculates the average and median final values, the proportion of paths that lose money and the 5th and 95th percentiles.
